# SPIKE Q1: are cinematic_control's offsets WORLD-space or DOLLY-LOCAL?
#
# The single most consequential unknown in CINEMATIC_PLAN.md. The engine's whole camera API
# is one call - camera at dollyID + dollyPos, looking at targetID + targetPos - and whether
# that offset is rotated into the dolly's frame decides what is buildable:
#
#   LOCAL  -> "over the left shoulder" is one call; chase and orbit shots are free.
#   WORLD  -> the offset is a fixed point in space; every chase or orbit must recompute an
#             anchor position every tick, and the mover in Phase 2 gets much heavier.
#
# The mock is known to be WORLD (it adds the offset raw), so it is the control case here.
# The ENGINE run is the experiment.
#
# THE DISCRIMINATOR: a ship yaws in place while the camera is pinned to it with a sideways
# offset. Watch the SHIP against the fixed rocks behind it:
#
#   the ship holds still and the rocks sweep past  -> LOCAL  (camera orbited with the ship)
#   the ship turns in front of you, rocks fixed    -> WORLD  (camera stayed put)

@map/visual_camera_offsets "Visual: camera offsets (spike Q1)"
" World-space or dolly-local? Watch the spinning ship against the fixed rocks.
    visual_set_index_by_name("camera_offsets")
    await task_schedule(run_visual_camera_offsets)
    ->END


=== run_visual_camera_offsets
metadata: ``` yaml
type: visual/camera_offsets
```
    # TWO subjects, because two different things can hide a spinning ship and I cannot tell
    # them apart from here:
    #   * an NPC renders for certain, but its own steering may own steer_yaw and overwrite it
    #     (the mock's _npcship_steer demonstrably does);
    #   * TERRAIN keeps whatever steer rate it is given - LM tumbles its asteroids this way -
    #     but terrain only ever carries asteroid / wreck / planet art in LM, so a ship hull on
    #     a terrain object may simply not draw.
    # Putting both in frame answers "which renders" and "which actually spins" in one look,
    # instead of me guessing and you re-running.
    _npc = to_object(npc_spawn(0 - 1100, 0, 0, "NPC hull", "tsn", "tsn_battle_cruiser", "behav_npcship"))
    _terr = to_object(terrain_spawn(1100, 0, 0, "TERRAIN hull", "#,spinner", "tsn_battle_cruiser", "behav_asteroid"))
    # A rock as the control: terrain + asteroid art certainly renders and certainly tumbles.
    _rock = to_object(terrain_spawn(0, 0, 1400, "ROCK", "#,spinner", "plain_asteroid_6", "behav_asteroid"))
    ->END if _npc is None and _terr is None

    if _npc is not None:
        visual_spin(_npc.id, seconds_per_turn=14)
    if _terr is not None:
        visual_spin(_terr.id, seconds_per_turn=14)
    if _rock is not None:
        visual_spin(_rock.id, seconds_per_turn=8)

    # Fixed background. Without a stationary reference you cannot tell which of the two moved.
    for _oi in range(9):
        terrain_spawn(0 - 6000 + _oi * 1500, 0, 5200, f"ref{_oi}", "#,ref", "plain_asteroid_9", "behav_asteroid")

    # Framed from an ANCHOR this pass, not from a subject: the question right now is which of
    # them is visible at all, and that must not depend on the camera riding one of them.
    _anchor = visual_anchor(0, 0, 0)
    visual_camera(_anchor, eye=(0, 1200, 0 - 4200))

    _expect = ~~[
        "LEFT: a cruiser spawned as an NPC. RIGHT: the same hull spawned as TERRAIN",
        "FRONT: a plain rock, the control - terrain art that certainly draws and tumbles",
        "which of the three can you SEE? a missing one cannot carry the Q1 test",
        "which of the three is SPINNING? an NPC's own steering may overwrite the rate",
        "the card reports the measured rate of whichever subject the camera is watching",
    ]~~
    visual_case("Camera offsets", _expect, subtitle="which subject renders AND spins - Q1 comes after", notes="framed from an anchor this pass, so visibility does not depend on the camera riding a subject", data="NPC hull left, terrain hull right, control rock in front; one turn per 14s")

    # MEASURE the spin and put the number on the card. The units of steer_yaw are documented
    # nowhere, so what matters is what the renderer actually did - and a subject that turns
    # too slowly to notice reads exactly like a world-space camera, which would make this
    # spike answer Q1 confidently and wrongly.
    _h0 = visual_heading(_rock.id)
    await delay_sim(3)
    _h1 = visual_heading(_rock.id)
    visual_case_note(visual_rate_text(_h0, _h1, 3))
    visual_expect("spike Q1: at least one hull spawned", _npc is not None or _terr is not None)
    visual_expect("spike Q1: rotating fast enough to see", visual_rate_value(_h0, _h1, 3) > 5, visual_rate_text(_h0, _h1, 3))
    ->END
